A fourth absence runs through the whole file and is of a different kind. The 428 delivered emails that drew no reply have no greeting at all, because a greeting exists only where a reply exists. Their greeting is not a value that was recorded and then lost, or one that a coder could not read, but an outcome that never came into being, and the problem that it raises, comparing greetings only among the emails that drew a reply, is a problem of design rather than of missing data. The [module on audit experiments](experiments.qmd#sec-posttreatment) and the [module on causal graphs](dags.qmd#sec-collider) take it up, and this page leaves it aside.

## 2. Why a value is missing {#sec-mechanisms}

What a missing value does to an estimate depends on why it is missing, and the vocabulary for the reasons goes back to @Rubin1976InferenceMissingData. In the review of @Little1992RegressionMissingXs, values are missing completely at random (MCAR) when the chance that a value is missing does not depend on any value in the data, observed or not, and missing at random (MAR) when it depends only on values that are observed (p. 1229). Values that are missing for reasons connected with what is missing, an income left blank because it is high, say, are missing not at random. Under MCAR the cases with complete data are a random subsample of all the cases, so an analysis of the complete cases alone loses precision but nothing else: "If the data are MCAR, listwise deletion will not introduce any bias into parameter estimates" [@Allison2009MissingData, p. 75], listwise deletion being the default of almost every statistical program, which drops every case with any missing value in the variables of a model.

An experiment adds a question that the three categories do not ask, namely whether the treatment could have changed whether a value is missing. @ZhaoDing2024AdjustNotAdjust put the point for covariates that are recorded before treatment: "the missingness indicators act as fully observed pretreatment covariates as long as missingness is not affected by the treatment" (abstract). A missingness indicator is a 0/1 variable that records, for each case, whether a value is missing, and the point of the sentence is that such an indicator, when it was settled before the treatment was assigned, is information about the cases like any other, which random assignment balances between the groups whether or not the values themselves are known. The last column but one of @tbl-kinds is there for this reason. The community thirds and the assessment third describe the authority and were recorded before any email went out, so the name cannot have changed whether the thirds are missing. A bounce is settled when the email fails to arrive, before any official could react to the name. The greeting codes are the exception, since the five uncoded replies were written by officials who had read the name, and whether a reply could be coded may in principle depend on what the name led the official to write.

## 3. Bounced emails {#sec-bounced}

Of the 1,014 emails sent, 23 bounced. They carry no reply status, since the codebook of the OAs records the reply as missing "if email bounced back due to email issue", and they never reached an official. The chunk below counts them by the group of the name and by whether the address was hard to find.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
audit |>
  datawizard::data_modify(bounced = as.integer(is.na(reply))) |>
  datawizard::data_summary(emails = n(), bounced = sum(bounced), share = 100 * mean(bounced), by = "group")
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
group    | emails | bounced | share
-----------------------------------
black    |    329 |       3 |  0.91
hispanic |    368 |      11 |  2.99
white    |    317 |       9 |  2.84
```


:::

