```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
[1] 0.1313879
```


:::
:::




The bounces are few and spread across the groups, at 2.8% of the emails with white names, 0.9% with Black names and 3.0% with Hispanic names, and a $\chi^2$ test finds no more difference between the groups than chance would produce (p = 0.131). For the name to have caused a bounce, a mail server would have had to refuse a message because of the name of its sender, which the counts cannot rule out but give no reason to suspect. A bounced email is therefore best read as an email to which the treatment was never delivered, for reasons settled by the address, and leaving the bounced emails out keeps the comparison among the delivered emails as random as the assignment of the names made it, and every specification of the curve leaves the bounced emails out.

The alternative, counting a bounced email as one that went unanswered, matters only when all emails are counted, since a bounced email is never a reply. Among all delivered emails to white and Hispanic names, with the uncoded replies counted as not named, the gap in greetings by name is −13.2 points. Counting the bounced emails as unanswered moves it to −12.8 points, a small change, but one produced by broken addresses rather than by officials, which is why the [specification menu](../cases/housing/menu.qmd#level-measurement) keeps the bounced emails off the curve.

## 4. Replies without a greeting code {#sec-uncoded}

The OAs coded the greeting of every reply but 5, and the file does not say why these five were left uncoded.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
audit |>
  datawizard::data_filter(reply == 1, is.na(named)) |>
  datawizard::data_select(c("email_id", "name", "group", "hard_email", "reply24"))
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
