Every strategy that keeps all 362 replies gives a gap close to the unadjusted −20.0 points, from −19.8 to −20.4, with standard errors of about 5.2 points. The complete-case analysis, by contrast, gives −17.2 points on 246 replies, with a larger standard error, and almost the whole of its departure is already present in the unadjusted gap among the same complete cases. The adjustment itself changes little. Dropping the authorities without a score changes the comparison, and it costs the precision that the adjustment was meant to buy.

Multiple imputation adds almost nothing here. The share of the variance of the gap that comes from the variation between the imputations, which mice reports as the fraction of missing information, is 0.009, because the name was assigned at random and so carries no information about the missing scores, whatever they are. In an experiment the category for the missing gives the same answer with no model of the missing values, which is the case made by @ZhaoDing2024AdjustNotAdjust for preferring it. Imputation earns its place where the variable with missing values is itself the object of the analysis rather than an aid to precision: a comparison of the gap across the thirds of percent Hispanic, which the [subgroups module](subgroups.qmd) sets out, cannot give the replies without a third a category of their own, and an observational comparison, such as one among the officials who replied, has no random assignment to fall back on. There, the observational case argued by @Allison2009MissingData and by @KingEtAl2001AnalyzingIncompletePolitical applies in full, and the plausibility of MAR, here a missing score explained by the size of the community and the other recorded columns, becomes the assumption on which the estimate depends.

## 6. Missing values on the curve {#sec-curve}

Each kind of missing information enters the curve of the case in its own way. The bounced emails are outside the experiment under every option, because no official read a bounced email and the name cannot have caused a bounce. The uncoded replies are a decision of the menu, left out or counted as not named, and the two extreme codings of section 4 show that no handling of them can change the conclusion. The community thirds are handled as complete cases, so the specifications that adjust for them lose 5 of the 362 coded replies of the reference comparison, and part of the shift between the adjusted and the unadjusted specifications, as the [specification menu](../cases/housing/menu.qmd#level-estimation) notes, comes from the rows that the adjustment loses rather than from the adjustment itself. A declared analysis can name a different handling, such as a category for the missing, provided that the declaration names it before the model is run, since a choice made after the estimates are known is one more of the forking paths of the [multiverse module](multiverse.qmd).

